     1	# CatchFn Specification (Draft)
     2	
     3	Last updated: 2026-01-14  
     4	Status: Draft (implementation not started)  
     5	Owner: Platform (Superfunctions)
     6	
     7	## Goal
     8	Create `catchfn`, a self-hosted bug capture and reporting layer (similar to Bugasura/Jam.dev) built on top of existing Superfunctions primitives. Focus on lightweight in-app reporting, automated context capture, issue syncing, and reuse of `watchfn` for ingestion/analysis.
     9	
    10	## Scope (v1)
    11	- In-app reporter widget (JS/browser) that lets users submit a bug with screenshot/annotation, description, steps, severity, tags.
    12	- Automatic context capture: URL, user/session, environment, console logs, network requests summary, breadcrumbs, device info.
    13	- Manual API: `catchFn().captureBug()` to programmatically submit bug reports.
    14	- Storage/transport: reuse `watchfn` ingestion/event router; introduce `bug_report` event type.
    15	- Issue sync: optional auto-create/link to GitHub/Linear via existing `botfn` integrations.
    16	- Attachments: inline screenshot (base64 or blob reference) and optional additional attachments (URLs or uploaded via `watchfn` file channel if available).
    17	
    18	## Persistence & Storage
    19	- Transport: reuse `watchfn` ingestion endpoint/auth/batching; no new transport surface.
    20	- Schema: extend `watchfn` event schema with `type: "bug_report"` and the data model above; validate required fields at ingest.
    21	- Storage: same datastore as other watchfn events; partition by `app`/`type`; indexes on (`type`, `app`, `timestamp`), (`type`, `app`, `report.severity`), (`integration.issueId`), (`sessionId`), (`replayId`).
    22	- Attachments: small images inline (data URL); large assets uploaded via watchfn’s file channel/blob adapter and stored as URLs in the event record.
    23	- Query: accessible via watchfn query API with filters for `type = bug_report`, severity, tags, time range, app.
    24	- Retention: default to watchfn retention; allow `bug_report`-specific TTL override and size caps (truncate console/network logs to last N).
    25	
    26	## Non-Goals (v1)
    27	- Full dashboard UI (rely on `watchfn` queries/filters for now).
    28	- Native mobile SDKs (web-only; React Native later).
    29	- Video/session replay (planned v1.1).
    30	- On-prem deployment changes (reuse existing `watchfn` deployment).
    31	
    32	## Personas & Use Cases
    33	- Frontend engineers: fast repro context when a user reports an issue.
    34	- QA: submit annotated repros during test runs; auto-file to GitHub/Linear.
    35	- Support: capture user-facing issues with environment + logs without dev tools.
    36	
    37	## Architecture
    38	- Client SDK (`catchfn/typescript/src`):
    39	  - Wraps/depends on `watchfn` browser client for transport and breadcrumbs.
    40	  - Reporter widget UI (vanilla + React helper) that triggers capture + UI overlay.
    41	  - Uses `drawfn` for screenshot annotation.
    42	- Event model:
    43	  - New event type `bug_report` in `watchfn` ingestion schema.
    44	  - Stored alongside other watch events; query via `watchfn` API.
    45	- Issue sync:
    46	  - Optional webhook/bridge to `botfn` persistence service to create/link issues (GitHub/Linear).
    47	  - Status sync via existing `botfn` jobs/hooks.
    48	- Session replay (v1.1):
    49	  - Plugin that records DOM mutations/redacted inputs; links `replayId` to bug report.
    50	
    51	## Data Model (`bug_report` event)
    52	- `id`: string (generated client-side)
    53	- `timestamp`: ISO string
    54	- `type`: "bug_report"
    55	- `app`: { name, version? }
    56	- `user`: { id?, email?, name? } (configurable; redaction supported)
    57	- `sessionId`: string
    58	- `context`: {
    59	  url,
    60	  locale?,
    61	  env?: "prod" | "staging" | "dev" | string,
    62	  release?: string,
    63	  featureFlags?: Record<string, boolean|string>,
    64	  viewport?: { width, height, dpr },
    65	  device?: { os, browser, ua },
    66	  network?: { downlink?, rtt?, effectiveType? },
    67	  console?: Array<{ level, message, ts }>,
    68	  networkLogs?: Array<{ method, url, status?, durationMs?, error? }>,
    69	  breadcrumbs?: Breadcrumb[],
    70	  custom?: Record<string, any>
    71	}
    72	- `report`: {
    73	  title: string,
    74	  description?: string,
    75	  steps?: string[],
    76	  severity?: "critical" | "high" | "medium" | "low",
    77	  tags?: string[],
    78	}
    79	- `attachments`: [
    80	  {
    81	    kind: "screenshot" | "file" | "link",
    82	    mimeType?: string,
    83	    dataUrl?: string,   # for small images (configurable size cap)
    84	    url?: string,       # for uploaded asset
    85	    annotationScene?: Scene  # drawfn scene for annotations
    86	  }
    87	]
    88	- `integration`: {
    89	  target?: "github" | "linear",
    90	  project?: string,
    91	  issueId?: string,     # when linked/created
    92	  issueUrl?: string
    93	}
    94	- `replayId?`: string (when session replay enabled)
    95	
    96	## Client API (TS)
    97	```ts
    98	import { catchFn } from "catchfn";
    99	
   100	const catcher = catchFn({
   101	  name: "my-app",
   102	  watch: { apiKey, endpoint },   // passed to watchfn init
   103	  user: () => ({ id: user.id, email: user.email }),
   104	  env: "prod",
   105	  enableConsoleCapture: true,
   106	  enableNetworkCapture: true,
   107	  beforeSend: (event) => event,  // allow redaction/drop
   108	  integrations: { github: { repo: "org/repo", token?: string }, linear: { teamId: "ENG" } },
   109	});
   110	
   111	// Manual capture
   112	await catcher.captureBug({
   113	  title: "Checkout button unresponsive",
   114	  description: "Clicks do nothing on Safari",
   115	  steps: ["Go to /checkout", "Click Pay"],
   116	  severity: "high",
   117	  tags: ["checkout", "safari"],
   118	});
   119	
   120	// Open reporter widget (end-user/QA flow)
   121	catcher.openReporter();
   122	```
   123	
   124	Key classes/modules:
   125	- `CatchFn`: main facade; holds watchfn client, config, reporters.
   126	- `ReporterWidget`: overlay UI; collects form inputs, captures screenshot, allows annotation via `drawfn`.
   127	- `ContextCollectors`: console log buffer, network interceptor (wraps fetch/XMLHttpRequest), device/env collectors; must be opt-in and size-capped.
   128	- `Uploader`: (optional) uploads large attachments to watchfn file channel or provided endpoint; falls back to dataURL for small images.
   129	
   130	## UI/UX (Reporter Widget)
   131	- Trigger: `catcher.openReporter()` or global hotkey (configurable) or floating button (opt-in).
   132	- Flow:
   133	  1) Capture screenshot of current viewport (html2canvas or browser capture API).
   134	  2) Annotate using `drawfn` overlay (arrows, boxes, text, blur).
   135	  3) Form: title (required), description, steps, severity, tags, checkbox to include console/network logs.
   136	  4) Submit -> calls `captureBug`; shows success + issue link if integration enabled.
   137	- Accessibility: focus trap, keyboard navigation, prefers-reduced-motion respect.
   138	
   139	## Transport & Ingestion
   140	- Reuse `watchfn` browser client; add `bug_report` event type.

